 add it to `odd_numbers`.\\n     - If `current` is even, set `current` to `current // 2`.\\n     - Otherwise, set `current` to `3 * current + 1`.\\n\\n3. **Final Check**:\\n   - If `1` is not already in `odd_numbers`, add it.\\n\\n4. **Sorting**:\\n\\n   - **Required implementation:** You must return the odd Collatz values in encounter order, starting from n and ending at 1, without sorting them. Preserve the existing sequence-generation and terminal-one handling.\\n\\n5. **Return**:\\n   - Return `odd_numbers`.\\n\\n#### Boundary Conditions\\n- **Input Validation**:\\n  - Ensure `n` is a positive integer.\\n- **Edge Cases**:\\n  - If `n` is 1, return `[1]`.\\n  - If `n` is even, include `n` in the list if it is odd.\\n  - The sequence will always contain at least one odd number, so an empty list is not possible.\\n\\n#### Complexity\\n- **Time Complexity**: O(log n) for the sequence generation and sorting.\\n- **Space Complexity**: O(log n) for storing the sequence.\\n\\nFollow this checklist to implement the function correctly."}'}]
